desc:Chariot [Arcana synth] (GFX)
//tags: instrument synth sequencer step sequencer phasing minimalist pulse marimba choir strings brass arcana
//author: JGWizrad / Garden FX (GFX)
//
// Requires gfx-drift, gfx-output, gfx-ui, gfx-panel, gfx-instrument and gfx-voice (.jsfx-inc) in the same folder.
//
// THE CHARIOT (card VII of the GFX Arcana series): momentum, control, the mechanical drive. A rapid minimalist
// phasing synth (inspired by the phase pieces of Steve Reich): two tracks play the same pattern; track B's clock
// is offset by a slowly, continuously variable PHASE SHIFT, so the two drift out of alignment and back again and new
// rhythmic tapestries appear between them. Relentless sixteenth and triplet pulsing; mallets, voices, strings, brass.
//   PATTERN    8 - 16 steps (LENGTH, default 12), edited on the grid: each step ON / ACCENT (click to cycle off, on,
//              accent; drag to paint), NOTE (1 - 8: which note of the HELD CHORD, counted up from the lowest; past
//              the notes held it wraps on up an octave) and OCT (-1 .. +2). With no chord held the pattern plays a
//              root - fifth - ninth cell on ROOT, so it can be auditioned. Click / drag to draw, the wheel steps a
//              value, a double-click (or a right-click) resets a step. CLEAR, ROTATE < >, and two template keys:
//              HANDS (a 12-beat clapping cell: 3 - 2 - 1 - 2) and CELL (twelve running notes).
//   CLOCK      RATE (a heavy stepped clock: 1/8, 1/8T, 1/16, 1/16T, 1/32), LENGTH (stepped, 8 - 16), ROOT, ACCENT (how
//              much louder and harder an accented step strikes), VELOCITY (how much the chord's key velocity counts).
//              RUN: locked to REAPER's transport while it plays, free-running at the project tempo while it is
//              stopped (to audition). TRANSPORT (lit by default): REAPER's play / record starts the run and its stop
//              stops it; RUN still works by hand. Off, the run ignores the transport.
//   PHASE      TRACK A plays the pattern on the clock; TRACK B plays the identical pattern on a clock offset by the
//              PHASE. PHASE SHIFT is a speed: how fast B drifts ahead (right) or behind (left) - from 1 step every
//              64 bars to 1 step every bar; in the centre it is LOCKED. The drift is smooth (B's notes slide off the
//              grid and come back onto it a step further on); STEP instead jumps B one whole step every N cycles of
//              the pattern (EVERY), the clapping way. PHASE: the offset you set by hand (drag B's ring on the scope,
//              or turn the knob); RESYNC snaps B back in line. While REAPER plays, the drift is reckoned from the
//              song position, so a render repeats.
//   TRACK A / B  each a voice family on lit keys - MALLETS, VOICES or STRINGS / BRASS - and LEVEL (fader), PAN (A
//              left, B right by default, as two pianos would sit), TONE (dark .. bright), OCTAVE (-2 .. +2).
//   MALLETS    a modal wooden bar (partials 1 : 3.9 : 9.2) over its tube resonator: HARDNESS (soft yarn to hard
//              rubber), DECAY (how long the bar rings), NOISE (the strike).
//   VOICES     formant choir voices: VOWEL (U, O, A, E, I), LENGTH (short stabs to sustained), SPREAD (the ensemble).
//   STRINGS / BRASS  a pulsed bowed-string / brass voice: BLEND (brass .. strings), SWELL (how each note swells
//              and recedes), LENGTH.
//   PAD        a sustained strings / brass layer (BLEND as above) on the held chord under both tracks, gated in
//              time by the PULSE: LEVEL (off in Init), TONE.
//   PULSE      the pulsing matrix: a gate locked to strict subdivisions (a stepped clock: 1/16, 1/16T, 1/32, 1/8T),
//              DEPTH, SHAPE (hard gate .. swell), SWING (0 by default). It always pulses the PAD; TRACKS lit, it
//              pulses tracks A and B's sustain too.
//   LFOS       one for each voice - TRACK A, TRACK B and the PAD - to bring it in and out of focus over long
//              stretches: SYNC (FREE, or one cycle every 1 - 128 bars; locked to the song position while REAPER plays),
//              RATE (free: 0.001 - 1 Hz, one cycle in about 17 minutes to one a second), SHAPE (sine, triangle, smooth
//              random, slewed sample & hold), PHASE (where it starts; A, B and the pad default 120 degrees apart), and
//              three depths: LEVEL (dips the voice down from where it is; 100 % fades it to silence at the trough),
//              PAN (sweeps it across the stereo field) and FILTER (its brightness: the mallets' hardness and tone, the
//              voices' upper formants, the strings / brass and pad low-pass). FATE breathes the free rates a little.
//              The scope's rings brighten and dim with the tracks' levels; a dot in each LFO's title shows where it is.
//   ROOM       a light hall: SIZE (its decay), MIX.
//   FATE       the series' drift (0 - 100 %, 50 % = the standard amount) as the performers' breath: each mallet
//              lands a few ms late or early and a little harder or softer, the two players' tuning wanders apart a
//              touch, and the drift rate itself breathes. The clock and the pulse never waver.
//   VOLUME, LEVEL, SAT (the output soft clip is always on).
// MIDI: notes (the held chord; channel select), CC120 / CC123 all notes off.

import gfx-drift.jsfx-inc
import gfx-output.jsfx-inc
import gfx-ui.jsfx-inc
import gfx-panel.jsfx-inc
import gfx-instrument.jsfx-inc
import gfx-voice.jsfx-inc

